  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 248 AM MST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\nThe weather pattern across eastern Idaho will remain benign \ntoday with high pressure remaining in control for one more day. \nEarly morning satellite imagery shows mostly clear skies \ncurrently although some patchy overnight fog could transpire in \na few localized spots over the next few hours. Strong \natmospheric subsidence and rising 500 mb heights will keep \nconditions dry and promote unseasonably mild afternoons, with \ndaytime highs running 7 to 12 degrees above seasonal averages in\nthe 50s and 60s. Forecasters will monitor boundary-layer mixing\nclosely to see if downslope flow pushes temperatures above the \nlow-to-mid 60s across portions of the Snake River Plain, Magic \nValley, and adjacent bench areas.\n\nThe tranquil synoptic pattern breaks down heading into the \nweekend as an upper-level trough over the Gulf of Alaska taps \ninto Pacific moisture and ejects energy across the Pacific \nNorthwest. Light precipitation will begin across the Wood River \nValley and Sawtooths later Friday evening, with the bulk of the \nmoisture falling from after midnight through late Saturday \nafternoon and early evening. Because the storm system \nsplits\u2014sending the primary low southeastward toward the Four \nCorners\u2014precipitation will be heavily concentrated across the \nWood River Valley, the Sawtooths and surrounding mountains, the \nSouth Hills/Albion Mountains, and the Southeast Highlands, while\nother locations see only light amounts, a tenth or two at most.\n\nFavored terrain and adjacent valleys are projected to receive \n0.25 to 0.50 inches of liquid or melted snow equivalent, with a \nfew spots across the highest elevations near Ketchum and the \nSouth Hills/Albion Mountains pushing 0.50 to 0.70 inches. QPF \namounts are well-aligned across deterministic and ensemble \nversions of the GFS and ECMWF aside from minor differences near \nthe Utah border. Snow levels will sit generally around 7,000 \nfeet, though elevated valleys such as the Stanley Basin will see\nlight accumulations. Overall snowfall totals will run up to 3 \ninches, with an additional 1 to 2 inches possible within \nlocalized heavier pockets. Thick cloud cover, precipitation, and\ncold advection will drop Saturday daytime highs sharply into \nthe 40s.\n \nEastern Idaho will reside in a brief lull between moisture \nsurges during the second half of the weekend. Aside from a few \nisolated showers lingering early in the day, conditions will \nremain mostly dry under partly cloudy skies until the next \nPacific low shifts inland. This break in precipitation will \nallow afternoon temperatures to rebound 5 degrees or so compared\nto Saturday's highs.\n\nA more pronounced and prolonged pattern shift takes hold to\nstart next week as broad upper-level troughing anchors over the\nwestern United States. While the National Blend of Models (NBM)\nintroduces precipitation as early as Sunday night, overall model\ntrends have favored holding off the bulk of Idaho's moisture\narrival until Monday. Synoptically, this incoming storm mirrors\nthe weekend system, featuring split flow with the main energy\npassing to our south. Once the low moves inland, temperatures\nwill drop sharply once again, ushering in below-normal readings\nand daily chances for scattered rain and mountain snow.\nDepending on the depth of cold air advection, snow levels could\nreach valley floors during overnight and early morning hours\ntoward the tail end of the forecast period, though considerable\nensemble spread remains regarding exact amounts and timing at\nthat range. Bottom line is next week continues to look much\ncooler and unsettled.",
